  "fact_notes": [
    "人物・出来事・制度は架空の教材例。実在の調査・統計・実証結果や法令ではない。制作台帳は来歴確認用。"
  ],
  "paragraphs": [
    {
      "id": "p1",
      "sentences": [
        {
          "en": "A fictional teacher prepares a lesson in which groups must explain why a paper bridge collapsed during a classroom activity.",
          "ja": "架空の教師は教室活動で紙の橋が崩れた理由を班で説明する授業を準備する。"
        },
        {
          "en": "Instead of distributing a model answer immediately, she provides photographs and notes from several attempts to build the bridge.",
          "ja": "すぐ模範解答を配らず複数の橋づくりの試行の写真とメモを提供する。"
        },
        {
          "en": "The students initially assume that every group must discover exactly the same explanation.",
          "ja": "当初生徒は全班が全く同じ説明を見つける必要があると想定する。"
        },
        {
          "en": "However, some observations remain ambiguous because the photographs were taken at different stages of construction.",
          "ja": "写真の撮影段階が異なるため曖昧な観察も残る。"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "p2",
      "sentences": [
        {
          "en": "One group argues that the distribution of weight caused the collapse.",
          "ja": "一班は重量の分布が崩壊の原因と主張する。"
        },
        {
          "en": "Another suggests that the shape of a supporting section might have contributed to the failure.",
          "ja": "別の班は支えの形が失敗に寄与したかもしれないと提案する。"
        },
        {
          "en": "The teacher asks each group to identify evidence supporting its explanation and describe an observation that might contradict it.",
          "ja": "各班に説明を支持する証拠と反するかもしれない観察を述べるよう求める。"
        },
        {
          "en": "Students discover that defending a hypothesis involves more than repeating a plausible statement with confidence.",
          "ja": "仮説の擁護は自信を持って妥当そうな文を繰り返す以上のことだと知る。"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "p3",
      "sentences": [
        {
          "en": "The class subsequently designs a limited experiment in which a single feature of the bridge is modified while other conditions remain as consistent as possible.",
          "ja": "その後橋の一特徴を変更し他条件をできるだけ一定にする限定実験を設計する。"
        },
        {
          "en": "They discuss why changing several variables simultaneously would make the resulting observations more difficult to interpret.",
          "ja": "複数の変数を同時に変えると結果の解釈が難しくなる理由を話し合う。"
        },
        {
          "en": "The teacher emphasizes that their hypothetical investigation cannot establish a universal engineering principle.",
          "ja": "仮想調査で普遍的な工学原理を確立できないと強調する。"
        },
        {
          "en": "Its purpose is to develop a method of comparing explanations rather than produce a definitive conclusion about every paper bridge.",
          "ja": "目的はすべての紙橋の決定的結論でなく説明を比較する方法を育てることだ。"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "p4",
      "sentences": [
        {
          "en": "For the final discussion, each group presents its original suggestion together with any revisions made after examining additional evidence.",
          "ja": "最終議論で各班は最初の案と追加根拠を調べた後の修正を示す。"
        },
        {
          "en": "Some students worry that changing an explanation might be regarded as an admission of intellectual weakness.",
          "ja": "説明変更が知的弱さの告白と見なされるか心配する生徒もいる。"
        },
        {
          "en": "The teacher explains that a well-supported revision can demonstrate a deeper understanding of the relationship between evidence and conclusions.",
          "ja": "根拠のある修正は証拠と結論の関係のより深い理解を示せると説明する。"
        },
        {
          "en": "She evaluates the clarity of reasoning separately from whether a group's first suggestion turned out to be correct.",
          "ja": "最初の案の正誤と別に推論の明確さを評価する。"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "p5",
      "sentences": [
        {
          "en": "Learning becomes more demanding when students must justify an explanation instead of simply recognizing a familiar answer.",
          "ja": "既知の答えを見つけるだけでなく説明を正当化する必要があると学習はより難しくなる。"
        },
        {
          "en": "Yet uncertainty can provide an opportunity to examine how knowledge is constructed.",
          "ja": "しかし不確実性は知識の構築方法を検討する機会になり得る。"
        },
        {
          "en": "A productive classroom allows students to change their minds for identifiable reasons without treating every opinion as equally valid.",
          "ja": "有益な教室は全意見を同等に妥当と扱わず明確な理由で考えを変えられる。"
        },
        {
          "en": "The lesson's value lies in the disciplined process through which the students learn to distinguish a possible explanation from a supported one.",
          "ja": "授業の価値は可能な説明と支持された説明を区別する規律ある過程にある。"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
